# mod_botshield test-rig sudoers snippet — OPTIONAL HARDENING.
#
# Background
# ----------
# The pytest test rig uses sudo for a handful of root-owned
# operations: editing the dev vhost config, reloading Apache,
# reading the dev error log, swapping MPMs, and removing
# state files. By default the test user has unrestricted sudo
# (typical of dev boxes), which means a compromised test
# dependency could escalate to root via the same sudo
# entitlement.
#
# This file scopes those entitlements to the EXACT commands and
# paths the test rig actually invokes. Drop it in as
# /etc/sudoers.d/botshield-tests (mode 0440, owner root) and the
# test rig will continue to function with no other privileges
# than what's listed below.
#
# Security review LOW #15 was the prompt to package this; the
# framework can't enforce sudoers scoping itself, but providing
# the exact ruleset makes it cheap for operators to opt in.
#
# Caveats
# -------
# - Replace `bsdev` below with the actual test user's username.
# - Re-audit on each release: if a new test grows new sudo calls,
#   they'll need to be added here. The full set of sudo
#   invocations in the test framework is grep'able from
#   tests/botshield_test/ + tests/pytests/.
# - These rules grant unauthenticated sudo (NOPASSWD) to the
#   listed commands — that's deliberate so pytest doesn't hang
#   on a TTY prompt. The protection comes from the path/argument
#   constraints, not from the password gate.

# --- Apache lifecycle ---
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl reload apache2
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl restart apache2

# --- MPM swap (test_mpm_matrix only) ---
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/a2enmod  mpm_event
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/a2enmod  mpm_worker
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/a2enmod  mpm_prefork
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/a2dismod mpm_event
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/a2dismod mpm_worker
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/a2dismod mpm_prefork

# --- vhost config edit (config_override) ---
# Stage temp file under same dir, atomic mv replaces the live
# config. The exact temp-name pattern matches Path(conf).parent /
# f".{Path(conf).name}.tmp" in tests/botshield_test/apache.py.
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/tee /etc/apache2/sites-available/.botshield-dev.conf.tmp
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/mv /etc/apache2/sites-available/.botshield-dev.conf.tmp /etc/apache2/sites-available/botshield-dev.conf

# --- Apache config-syntax check (test_directive_validation) ---
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/apachectl -C * -t

# --- State-file ops (test_state_save / test_persist) ---
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/rm -f /var/lib/botshield/state.bin

# --- Secret-rotation tests (test_secret_rotation) ---
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/install -m 0600 -o root /tmp/.bs-secret-rotation-* /etc/botshield/secret-secondary
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/install -m 0600 -o root /tmp/.bs-secret-rotation-* /etc/botshield/secret
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/rm -f /etc/botshield/secret-secondary

# --- Log inspection (log_slice fixture) ---
# Grant read-only access to the two error logs the tests slice.
# An alternative would be `chgrp adm` + `chmod 640` on those
# files — that eliminates these sudo entries entirely. Pick
# whichever fits your operational policy.
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/cat /var/log/apache2/botshield-dev-error.log
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/cat /var/log/apache2/error.log
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/grep -c * /var/log/apache2/botshield-dev-error.log
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/grep -c * /var/log/apache2/error.log
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/stat -c %s /var/log/apache2/botshield-dev-error.log
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/stat -c %s /var/log/apache2/error.log
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/journalctl -u apache2*

# --- File-size probe (used in a couple of tests) ---
bsdev ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/stat -c %s /etc/botshield/*
